## Final experiment design

### Title
"Факторная оценка критичности динамического lookup и представления единиц памяти в InfLLM на длинноконтекстных задачах"

### 1. Research rationale & hypotheses

Background: InfLLM (training-free long-context extrapolation) segments context into memory units; each unit has a representation; at decode time the query attends to unit representations, selects top-k units, and attends to their tokens plus a recent window. Two components determine what the model "sees" from the distant context: (i) the mechanism that chooses which units to attend (dynamic top-k lookup), and (ii) the quality of the unit representations that drive that selection. The question: how critical is each to final task performance?

Because InfLLM is training-free and inference-only, both factors are pure inference-time knobs. The design isolates them.

Hypotheses:
- H1 (lookup criticality): Replacing dynamic top-k lookup with random unit selection (same token budget) significantly drops performance on tasks whose needed information is in distant context (early/mid needles; remote-memory LongBench items); effect near zero on recent-window-covered items (late needles). I.e., main effect of A large for remote-need subsets, small for local-need subsets.
- H2 (representation criticality): Representation choice affects performance through selection quality (hit-rate), mostly under dynamic lookup; effect is smaller than A's main effect but non-negligible for compact/corrupted representations. I.e., main effect of B moderate; interaction A×B expected: representation matters more under dynamic than random (because under random, selection quality is irrelevant by construction — random selection bypasses the representation entirely). Actually under random, representation has essentially no role in selection (it chooses uniformly); representation still irrelevant to attended tokens (attention on tokens uses normal KV, not the rep). So we expect B effect ≈ 0 under A3 random, and > 0 under A1 dynamic. This interaction is itself a key result: it tells us the representation only matters to the extent that lookup uses it.
- H3 (mechanism mediation): variation across B conditions correlates with top-k hit-rate; hit-rate mediates the effect of B on scores under dynamic lookup.
- H4 (ceiling calibration): Even the best selective condition (A1/B1) stays below full-attention ceiling on remote-need subsets — measuring how much loss the selection paradigm inherently costs (expected, since InfLLM is a sparse approximation).

